"""mtools on Bazel — the warrants ARE the build graph, and a test IS a witness.

Two distributions (`mikemol-mdstruct`, `mikemol-hooks`), each a paperkit PROJECT: its claims live in
`warrants.bib`, each claim's `check` is a witness that exits 0, and its prose document is PROJECTED
from those claims rather than written beside them. So the documentation and the gate are one graph —
a paragraph that cites `[@key]` cannot survive its claim going red.

⚑⚑⚑ A PYTEST CASE AND A PAPERKIT WITNESS ARE THE SAME OBJECT (operator). Both are a named assertion
that exits 0/1 and carries prose saying what it establishes and why it must exist. mtools' 116 cases
already carry that prose — every one has a `⚑` docstring arguing the defect it prevents — so they
were witnesses written in the wrong frame, not tests needing a second layer wrapped round them.

⚑⚑ PAPERKIT ARRIVES AS A PUBLISHED WHEEL, NOT AS A BAZEL MODULE OR A VENDORED TREE (operator:
*"paperkit will be published as a package. Same as the mtools projects. Best to install paperkit
into sandbox .venv in the same way."*). It is a real dependency in each distribution's
`pyproject.toml`, resolved through `pip.parse` below, so Bazel stages the RESOLVED WHEEL as a
declared input to every action that needs it.

⚑⚑ AND THAT IS WHY THE WHEEL WORKS WHERE AN EDITABLE INSTALL DOES NOT. paperkit's own
`tools/verb.bzl` records the defect it hit: *"`.venv` is a directory of POINTERS at host absolute
paths … the editable install's finder carries `MAPPING = {'paperkit': '/home/…/paperkit'}`. Staged
into a sandbox that would not dangle — it would RESOLVE, out of the sandbox, to the live working
tree, and every check would read unstaged sources and report green. A sandbox escape that passes is
worse than one that fails."* A PUBLISHED wheel carries no such pointer: it is real files under
`site-packages`, content-addressed and relocatable. The distinction is not incidental — it is the
whole reason the operator's answer is publish-and-install rather than consume-in-place.

⚑ THIS REPO ALSO CARRIED THAT DEFECT IN MINIATURE. `mikemol-mdstruct` was installed editable, its
`.pth` + import-hook finder invisible to mypy (which reads the filesystem, not `sys.path` hooks), so
every test typed as `Any` while 90 of them passed. The repair was `mypy_path`/`explicit_package_bases`
— a patch AROUND the mechanism. Under rules_python the runfiles tree resolves imports BY LAYOUT and
that patch retires: ⟡mtools-drop-mypypath.
"""

module(name = "mtools", version = "0.1")

# ⚑ A HERMETIC PYTHON — "which python" is Bazel's, not the host's. Same version the distributions
# declare (`requires-python = ">=3.13"`), so the interpreter that runs a witness is the interpreter
# the package claims to support rather than whatever the caller's shell resolved.
bazel_dep(name = "rules_python", version = "1.0.0")

# ⚑ `@platforms` IS DECLARED BECAUSE //rbe NAMES ITS CONSTRAINTS. It arrives transitively for many
# graphs, but a transitive repository is not a VISIBLE one under bzlmod — measured, `@platforms//os`
# resolved to "unknown repo requested from @@" until this line existed. A dependency a file
# references by name is a dependency that file's module must declare.
bazel_dep(name = "platforms", version = "0.0.10")
python = use_extension("@rules_python//python/extensions:python.bzl", "python")
python.toolchain(python_version = "3.13", is_default = True)

# ⚑⚑ THE DEPENDENCY SET IS DECLARED, NOT INHERITED FROM A VENV. `pip.parse` resolves each
# distribution's requirements into Bazel repos, so a wheel is a STAGED INPUT with a content hash —
# not a directory the action happens to find on the host. This is what makes a `sandbox`-tier
# verdict mean anything: the action cannot read a package nobody declared.
#
# ⚑ ONE HUB PER DISTRIBUTION, because they are separate distributions with separate dependency
# sets. `mikemol-hooks` declares ruff+mypy as RUNTIME deps (its whole function is invoking them);
# `mikemol-mdstruct` declares panflute. Merging them into one hub would let a hooks witness import
# panflute because a sibling happened to need it — the same over-admission a shared venv gives.
pip = use_extension("@rules_python//python/extensions:pip.bzl", "pip")
pip.parse(
    hub_name = "mdstruct_deps",
    python_version = "3.13",
    requirements_lock = "//mdstruct:requirements.txt",
)
pip.parse(
    hub_name = "hooks_deps",
    python_version = "3.13",
    requirements_lock = "//hooks:requirements.txt",
)
pip.parse(
    hub_name = "ratchet_deps",
    python_version = "3.13",
    requirements_lock = "//ratchet:requirements.txt",
)

# ⚑⚑⚑ A SECOND LOCK PER DISTRIBUTION, FOR THE WITNESS ACTIONS ONLY. The shipping lock above
# excludes the dev group, so it cannot supply pytest — and the thesis at the top of this file is
# that A PYTEST CASE AND A PAPERKIT WITNESS ARE THE SAME OBJECT, which means the action staging a
# witness MUST have the runner. Merging pytest into the shipping lock would answer that by
# shipping a test runner to every consumer, which is the over-admission `pip.parse`'s one-hub-per-
# distribution split exists to refuse. Two locks, two hubs: what a consumer installs and what a
# witness runs under are different questions with different answers.
pip.parse(
    hub_name = "hooks_dev",
    python_version = "3.13",
    requirements_lock = "//hooks:requirements-dev.txt",
)
pip.parse(
    hub_name = "mdstruct_dev",
    python_version = "3.13",
    requirements_lock = "//mdstruct:requirements-dev.txt",
)
pip.parse(
    hub_name = "ratchet_dev",
    python_version = "3.13",
    requirements_lock = "//ratchet:requirements-dev.txt",
)
use_repo(
    pip,
    "hooks_deps",
    "hooks_dev",
    "mdstruct_deps",
    "mdstruct_dev",
    "ratchet_deps",
    "ratchet_dev",
)

# ⚑⚑⚑ pandoc IS A DECLARED INPUT NOW, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT FORCED IT: 54 OF 97 mdstruct CASES
# WERE SILENTLY SKIPPING. They carry a `needs_pandoc` marker whose `conftest` skips when
# `shutil.which("pandoc")` is None — correct on a host that lacks it, and a LIE inside a sandbox
# that lacks it BY CONSTRUCTION. `bazel test //...` reported 23 of 23 targets passing while more
# than half of one distribution's cases never executed, because a skipped case and a passing one
# are indistinguishable in that summary line. That is this repository's own defect class, sitting
# inside the hermetic suite meant to be the strongest thing here.
#
# ⚑⚑ FETCHED BY HASH FROM UPSTREAM, NOT VENDORED FROM THE HOST. The host binary is a statically
# linked ELF and would have staged cleanly, which is exactly why taking it would have been the
# wrong move: an input copied off the developing machine is a fact about that machine, and its
# provenance stops being answerable the moment the machine changes. The sha256 below is over the
# published release archive.
#
# ⚑ THE VERSION IS PINNED AND MATCHES WHAT THE HOST RUNS TODAY (3.8.3), so the hermetic result and
# the host result are comparable. A drift between them is a real finding rather than a mystery.
http_archive = use_repo_rule("@bazel_tools//tools/build_defs/repo:http.bzl", "http_archive")
http_archive(
    name = "pandoc",
    build_file_content = """exports_files(["bin/pandoc"])
filegroup(
    name = "bin",
    srcs = ["bin/pandoc"],
    visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
)
""",
    sha256 = "c224fab89f827d3623380ecb7c1078c163c769c849a14ac27e8d3bfbb914c9b4",
    strip_prefix = "pandoc-3.8.3",
    url = "https://github.com/jgm/pandoc/releases/download/3.8.3/pandoc-3.8.3-linux-amd64.tar.gz",
)

# ⚑⚑⚑ shellcheck IS DECLARED BECAUSE THE GATE'S OWN SHELL WAS UNGATED. `.githooks/pre-commit` is
# the only shell file this repository owns, it is the thing that runs every other check, and
# NOTHING CHECKED IT. It was clean only because its author ran shellcheck by hand after each edit
# — a convention, held by one person's memory, guarding the file that guards everything else.
#
# ⚑⚑ EXCLUDE IS EMPTY AND STAYS EMPTY UNTIL A WAIVER IS MEASURED. Two peer repositories carry
# three waived codes each; none transferred, because a false-positive population is measured in
# the tree that has it rather than inherited from a tree that had it. The one finding this repo
# has actually declined — SC2126, suggesting `grep -c` where the aggregate across files is the
# point — is argued in the hook itself, at the line it concerns.
#
# ⚑ SAME VERSION THE HOST RUNS (0.11.0), so a hermetic verdict and a developer's verdict are
# comparable, and a drift between them is a finding rather than a mystery.
http_archive(
    name = "shellcheck",
    build_file_content = """exports_files(["shellcheck"])
filegroup(
    name = "bin",
    srcs = ["shellcheck"],
    visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
)
""",
    sha256 = "8c3be12b05d5c177a04c29e3c78ce89ac86f1595681cab149b65b97c4e227198",
    strip_prefix = "shellcheck-v0.11.0",
    url = "https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/releases/download/v0.11.0/shellcheck-v0.11.0.linux.x86_64.tar.xz",
)

# ⚑⚑⚑ ruff IS AN http_archive AND NOT A pip DEPENDENCY, BECAUSE rules_python STAGES ONLY
# `site-packages`. The wheel's `bin/ruff` is dropped, so the installed package is a Python shim
# whose `find_ruff_bin()` looks for a binary that is not there — measured: `python -m ruff` inside
# a hermetic action raised, listing the paths it had searched. A pip requirement that cannot be
# EXECUTED is not a usable dependency for a test that runs the checker.
#
# ⚑⚑ AND THE GENERAL RULE, WHICH THIS REPOSITORY REACHED BY TRIAL AND A PEER REACHED BY KNOWING IT.
# rules_python ships `@rules_python//python/entry_points:py_console_script_binary.bzl`, which
# REGENERATES a console script from the wheel's own `entry_points.txt` — so a dropped `bin/` is not
# a wall for any tool that declares one. The discriminator is a one-command measurement:
#
#     importlib.metadata.distribution(X).entry_points   -> console_scripts group
#
#   · non-empty  -> py_console_script_binary. mypy declares dmypy/mypy/mypyc/stubgen/STUBTEST;
#     pytest declares pytest; panflute declares panfl/panflute.
#
#     ⚑ AND STUBTEST WAS EVALUATED AGAINST THAT RULE AND DECLINED, WHICH IS THE RULE WORKING.
#     The stub-authority witness invokes `python -m mypy.stubtest` and carries two workarounds: a
#     `MYPYPATH`, and a writable cwd because stubtest opens a sqlite metastore in `.mypy_cache`
#     relative to it. A launcher looked like it would retire them. MEASURED: running the console
#     script DIRECTLY still writes `.mypy_cache` into cwd, so the workaround is unaffected — and
#     `mypy` is already a declared dev dependency, so the module form resolves and the witness
#     passes REMOTELY as it stands. The launcher is the right instrument when a tool cannot
#     otherwise be executed; it is a refactor with no measured gain when the tool already runs.
#   · EMPTY and bin/ is a compiled artifact -> http_archive is correct. MEASURED for ruff:
#     `entry_points == []` and `file .venv/bin/ruff` reports an ELF executable. There is no
#     entry point to regenerate, which is why the archive below is the right instrument rather
#     than a workaround for not knowing the other one.
#
# ⚑ A PAIRED TRAP, RECORDED BEFORE IT IS HIT: a console-script binary must be consumed through
# `ctx.actions.run_shell(tools = ...)`, never `inputs = ...`. Its launcher looks for a
# `<bin>.runfiles/` tree beside the executable; `inputs` stages the flat files without that tree
# and the launcher raises "Cannot find .runfiles directory".
#
# ⚑⚑ THE VERSION IS THE ONE THE LOCK PINS (0.16.6), and `test_bar_fires` asserts that equality.
# Two sources for one tool is a place they can drift; the assertion is what makes a drift a
# finding rather than a silent divergence between what the gate runs and what a developer runs.
http_archive(
    name = "ruff",
    build_file_content = """exports_files(["ruff"])
filegroup(
    name = "bin",
    srcs = ["ruff"],
    visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
)
""",
    sha256 = "0696335ef16615d8c7445ad438750eb0f55b3da6f153df21265a7c6d5750254f",
    strip_prefix = "ruff-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu",
    url = "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/releases/download/0.16.6/ruff-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz",
)

# ⚑⚑⚑ ⟡mtools-mypy-in-the-graph — MYPY IS NOT AN ACTION HERE, AND THE COST IS INVALIDATION, NOT
# SPEED. `.githooks/pre-commit` runs `mypy` once per distribution over the whole tree, outside
# bazel, so its verdict is keyed on NOTHING: it re-runs in full every commit and no result is ever
# reused or invalidated. The two `dev_requirement("mypy")` lines in the BUILD files exist only so
# the stub-authority witness can import it.
#
# ⚑⚑ AND THE REASON A PEER SPLITS IT PER FILE IS TRANSITIVE REVALIDATION, WHICH IS A CORRECTNESS
# PROPERTY RATHER THAN A PERFORMANCE ONE (operator). mypy's verdict on a file depends on the TYPES
# of everything it imports, so a per-file action keyed on only its own file serves a STALE GREEN
# the moment a dependency's signature changes. MEASURED HERE, one edit:
#
#     spans.py alone                          Success: no issues found
#     change a return type in ast.py, which spans.py imports:
#       ast.py                                Found 1 error
#       spans.py (NOT EDITED)                 Found 1 error
#
# The unchanged file's verdict flipped. A cache key that did not contain `ast.py` would have
# served the earlier green — the same crossing-a-boundary-it-was-not-computed-under class this
# repository already refuses for sandbox flags, arriving through the KEY instead of the sandbox.
#
# ⚑ SO THE ADOPTABLE PART IS THE CLOSURE, NOT THE FAN-OUT. linux-sources generates 262 per-file
# targets, each declaring "the file PLUS its transitive first-party import closure — which only
# `import_closure` can compute ... a hand-maintained data list silently under-covers the import
# graph the moment an import is added", and its generator FAILS CLOSED, raising rather than
# emitting a target whose closure is indeterminate. mtools has 26 first-party modules with real
# intra-package imports (cli.py pulls seven; spans is imported by three), so the closure is not
# trivial here either. What is NOT established is whether per-file granularity earns its generator
# at this size, or whether one action per distribution — declaring that distribution's closure —
# gets the invalidation without the machinery. That is a measurement, not a preference.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ AND THE GENERAL FORM, WHICH NAMES EVERY DEFECT THIS REPOSITORY FOUND TODAY AS ONE THING:
# EVERY ACTION MUST BE Π-TYPED (operator). An action's output type DEPENDS ON its inputs, so the
# declared inputs must be the full domain the verdict ranges over. `mypy(spans.py)` is not a
# function of `spans.py`; it is a function of `spans.py` and its import closure. Declaring only
# the file types the action at the WRONG DOMAIN — and the cache is then perfectly correct about a
# function nobody meant. A stale green is not a caching bug; it is a type error one level up.
#
# The four found this session are the same shape, differing only in what escaped the domain:
#
#     test_bar_fires   declared staged sources    · reached the host .venv via resolve()
#     pandoc cases     declared the test module   · reached a host binary, or silently skipped
#     mypy(file)       declared one file          · ranges over its transitive closure
#     any action       declared its inputs        · reached the whole source tree, pre-hermetic
#
# ⚑⚑ WHICH IS WHY THE TWO REPAIRS ARE NOT ALTERNATIVES. Widening the declaration (stage pandoc,
# stage the closure) makes the domain match the function. Narrowing what is REACHABLE (the
# hermetic sandbox, the remote executor) makes the function unable to exceed its domain. The first
# is a statement; the second is an enforcement, and an undeclared dependency that cannot be
# reached is one that cannot be forgotten.
#
# ⚑⚑⚑ AND THIS IS WHY THE PEER REPOSITORIES CARRY SO MUCH CUSTOM BAZEL AND BAZEL-GENERATION CODE
# (operator). It is not elaboration and it is not scale. A STOCK RULE CANNOT EXPRESS A COMPUTED
# DOMAIN. `py_test` declares a `srcs`/`deps` list a human wrote; `mypy(f)` ranges over
# `f + closure(f)`, which is derived FROM THE SOURCE, so no hand-written list can state it and any
# that tries under-covers the instant an import is added. That gap is what `gen_gate_build.py`
# fills — and why it RAISES on an indeterminate closure rather than emitting a target keyed on a
# guess. paperkit's `pk_cmd` is the same problem for an arbitrary shell string, whose domain is
# likewise not expressible as `srcs`.
#
# ⚑⚑ SO THE SURVEY QUESTION "WHAT WOULD THIS BUY AT 25 TARGETS" WAS THE WRONG QUESTION, and this
# repository asked it twice. Twenty-five uniform `py_test`s are not evidence that mtools does not
# need a custom rule; they are evidence that EVERY CHECK HERE IS ONE WHOSE DOMAIN A HUMAN COULD
# WRITE DOWN. The first check whose domain is COMPUTED — a per-file type verdict, a witness over a
# closure, a shell string — will be typed wrongly by a stock rule, and the wrongness will present
# as a stale green rather than as an error. The trigger for building a rule is a computed domain,
# not a target count.
